+# Decision: Concurrency policy for independent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot-visit independently and then compare notes. A visit (plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes) is edited offline on shared tablets that can be disconnected for up to 48 hours, so two workers routinely start from the same fetched state and submit differing versions of the same visit. The record store must decide what a *current* visit means when such submissions arrive, and how a coordinator settles the difference.
+
+The choice sets the shape of everything that touches a visit: the submission envelope the tablets send, the metadata the server retains, the review work two coordinators perform, and which visit states may enter the Friday CSV. It is made here because neither the HLD nor the site catalog settles it, and because tablet-sync and review-export cannot be designed until it is fixed.
+
+Two invariants hold regardless of the policy and so are background, not deciding factors: no original submitted payload is ever discarded, and the server never treats arrival order as authority.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Safety against silently combining scientifically related edits (major).** Some edits are scientifically coupled — a revised species identification and its cover estimate, or a species entry and a note explaining it — while others are plainly independent, such as correcting a misspelled observer name. A policy that combines changes by field cannot see the coupling the field names don't express, so it can produce a composite record no observer actually submitted. This criterion turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and the non-goal *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*.
+- **Coordinator review workload (moderate).** Two coordinators handle roughly 6,000 visits per season with no dedicated administrator. Every visit sent to review is manual work; a policy that routes scientifically irrelevant differences (an observer-name fix) to review spends that budget. Turns on *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden (moderate).** The team has no dedicated systems administrator. Merge logic for scalars, observer lists, and species-entry collections is code that must be written, maintained, and re-validated as the record schema evolves. Turns on the same operability tenet.
+- **Inspectability from submission to exported value (moderate).** A coordinator and the science partner must be able to trace an exported value back to what a worker actually submitted. Turns on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Tablet client state and submission footprint (minor).** Whatever the tablet must retain and transmit per visit to make the policy work. Visit records are small structured documents, so this is a minor consideration at this scale.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries a complete visit payload and names the server-assigned **base revision** it was derived from. A submission whose base is the visit's sole current revision extends the chain and becomes the new current state. A submission whose base is no longer the sole current revision is kept as an additional **tip**: the visit now has competing complete revisions and is **unresolved**. A coordinator resolves it by selecting one competing revision — exactly as submitted — as the visit's current state; a resolution record names the coordinator, the competing revisions, and the winner. The server never combines fields from different submissions; a coordinator who wants a corrected visit submits a new revision based on one of the competitors.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and Worker B both fetch revision R0. A submits R1 (based on R0) — fast-forward, R1 is current. B, still offline, submits R2 (also based on R0). R0 already has a successor, so R2 is a second tip; the visit is unresolved with competing revisions {R1, R2}. A coordinator selects R1; R2 is superseded but retained in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Safety against silently combining coupled edits: **strong** — no submission is ever merged with another; a current visit is always exactly one worker's submitted record.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak** — every non-fast-forward submission becomes review work, including edits to unrelated fields that carry no scientific disagreement.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — the server compares base revisions and tracks tips; there are no field-type merge rules to maintain.
+- Inspectability from submission to exported value: **strong** — an exported value traces to a single named revision, author, and receipt time.
+- Tablet client state and submission footprint: **partial** — the tablet retains one base revision ID per visit and resends the full payload each submission.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger coordinator review queue for the guarantee that no record is ever silently assembled from parts.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state and the specific field changes it makes. When two submissions derive from the same base and change disjoint fields, the server combines them automatically into one record; only overlapping changes to the same field are routed to a coordinator. This needs per-type merge rules — scalars, the observer list, and the species-cover collection each merge differently — and field-level provenance so each field of a combined record records which submission it came from.
+
+**Demonstration.** From base R0, Worker A changes only the lead observer's name and Worker B adds a missing species row. The fields are disjoint, so the server produces one record carrying A's observer correction and B's new species, with no coordinator involvement. Only if both had edited the *same* species row would a coordinator see it.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Safety against silently combining coupled edits: **weak** — disjoint-at-the-field-level is not the same as scientifically independent; the rules combine a revised identification in one submission with a cover estimate from another without recognizing the two belong together, and no field name tells them apart.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** — only genuine same-field overlaps reach a coordinator, so unrelated corrections clear automatically and more visits are export-ready.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — merge rules for each field type are code to write, maintain, and re-validate whenever the record schema changes, against the no-administrator constraint.
+- Inspectability from submission to exported value: **partial** — provenance is recoverable field by field, but an exported record may be a composite that no single worker submitted, so the trace is per-field rather than to one submission.
+- Tablet client state and submission footprint: **partial** — the tablet retains a base snapshot and computes and sends structured per-field changes rather than a whole payload.
+
+**Summary.** Trades maintained merge machinery and composite records for a markedly smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions is chosen.** It wins on the one **major** criterion — safety against silently combining scientifically related edits — which the team weights above the others because an auto-combined record that crosses a scientific relationship is a wrong observation that nothing in the pipeline flags, whereas the cost field-aware merging saves is review time that the whole-visit policy merely spends rather than loses. Field-aware merging is the stronger option on review workload and comparable on footprint, but those are moderate and minor; it is weak exactly where it matters most, and its merge rules are standing maintenance against the no-administrator constraint. The decision turns on *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and the non-goal *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*; it is taken knowingly *against* the lean toward low coordinator workload.
+
+**Why the rejected option stays on the table.** Field-aware merging is not wrong in general — it is the right answer for a different balance of forces, and the conditions that would flip this decision are concrete. If the review queue proves unsustainable for two coordinators (for example, if the volume drives rubber-stamping, which would quietly erase the very safety advantage that justified the choice), the documented fallback is to introduce field-aware auto-combination **restricted to fields that are reliably scientifically independent** — an observer-name correction is the clear case — while still routing every species or cover change to review. Re-running this judgment then means re-weighing review workload as the now-dominant constraint and accepting the merge-rule maintenance cost deliberately. This doc holds the options and criteria so that re-run does not start from scratch.
+
+**What committing forecloses.** The record store offers no field-combining operation, including at resolution; a coordinator cannot assemble a visit from parts of several submissions, only select one whole revision (or submit a new one). Edits to unrelated fields of the same visit still cost a review even when there is no scientific disagreement.
+
+**What committing obligates downstream** (cascade notes — these nodes are designed in later sessions and own these mechanisms):
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID of each locally cached visit and name it on its next submission; resend the whole visit payload (not a diff) with an idempotency key; and surface to the field worker when the server reports a submission left the visit unresolved, so the worker understands their edit is awaiting coordinator resolution rather than lost.
+- **Review-export** must present a comparison of the competing complete revisions for a visit (record-store supplies the revisions and history; the comparison screen is review-export's), call the resolution operation with the selected and competing revision IDs, hold unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV until they are resolved, and carry resolution provenance (coordinator identity, contributing submission IDs) so an exported value remains traceable to the submission it came from.
